WEBVTT

00:00:11.780 --> 00:00:30.660
<v Ira Glass>This is not a setting where Chris was used to learning anything important, much less having his whole world rocked by something somebody said. He was 15, in health class, in San Juan Capistrano, California. As Chris remembers it, it was the beginning of the period. Class was just beginning to settle down. The teacher was also the school's basketball coach.

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:48.870
<v Chris Benderev>You know, in my memory, he's the standard-issue-- I don't know-- tallish, white guy coach with, like, neatly parted brown hair. And the bell rings. Class is supposed to start. And we're all just talking over him, not listening.

00:00:48.870 --> 00:01:03.860
<v Chris Benderev>And he's trying to get class started. And I think he's getting, understandably, a little annoyed. And then at one point, one of the girls said loudly, we're all going to be friends forever.

00:01:03.860 --> 00:01:38.890
<v Chris Benderev>And then he gets our attention and said something like, just for your information, you're not going to all stay friends forever. And let me tell you a little bit about how friendships work. He says, in a couple of years, high school is going to end, and you're going to all scatter to different jobs or colleges, and you're going to start falling out of touch with each other, and eventually, you're not going to talk to most of any of these other people. And now, you might--

00:01:38.890 --> 00:01:48.440
<v Ira Glass>(LAUGHING) Can we just pause? That is such-- it's real. It's very true. But what a funny thing to say to a bunch of kids.

00:01:48.440 --> 00:01:59.940
<v Chris Benderev>Yeah. It's like, suddenly, he had our full attention.

00:01:59.940 --> 00:02:10.480
<v Ira Glass>But then the teacher kept going. He wasn't done. He got very specific and said, OK, you might stay in touch with a few friends from high school.

00:02:10.480 --> 00:02:32.800
<v Chris Benderev>He said, like, well, then you're going to get into your 30s and your 40s, and it'll be harder. You'll be working. And then some of you might get married, and your free time-- a lot of that should go towards your spouse. And if you have kids, oh, whatever little free time you have left, that'll go to the kids.

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:52.510
<v Chris Benderev>And finally, he said, the only friends you're going to be left with are the parents of whatever kid your little toddler or whatever randomly sidles up to because they both like the same part of the playground. That person, that parent-- that's going to be your friend.

00:02:52.510 --> 00:03:04.830
<v Ira Glass>That is a very thorough and vivid and not inaccurate picture of the future, that it's amazing that he went into that much detail.

00:03:04.830 --> 00:03:30.150
<v Chris Benderev>Yes. By then, we were like-- I remember a sort of, like, stunned silence at that point. And maybe there's one person who said, like, no, or like, no, he's wrong, or we're going to stay friends, or something. And then the class-- and then the class began, and I don't remember anything else from that day.

00:03:30.150 --> 00:03:39.540
<v Ira Glass>This is actually one of the producers on our show, Chris Benderev. And he says he remembers the other kids in class, kind of, shrugging this off like, yeah, whatever. But he couldn't.

00:03:39.540 --> 00:03:42.210
<v Ira Glass>Did you think it was true?

00:03:42.210 --> 00:04:00.930
<v Chris Benderev>Absolutely. I thought that he-- I remember thinking, oh, no, I hope that he's wrong, but it sounds like he's right. That's what I remember thinking. It had the air of truth, partly because it was so specific-- the playground detail, especially.

00:04:00.930 --> 00:04:15.940
<v Ira Glass>Before this moment, Chris hadn't bothered picturing what the future was going to be very much. He had a vague sense that things were going to get better and better. But now, thanks to this random speech by this otherwise forgotten teacher, he realized the future he was facing--

00:04:15.940 --> 00:04:29.500
<v Chris Benderev>It's going to get tedious and small and narrow and boring. Because when you're in high school, what is better than hanging out with your friends?

00:04:29.500 --> 00:04:29.800
<v Ira Glass>Right.

00:04:29.800 --> 00:04:45.660
<v Chris Benderev>Like, that was the best thing you could do. And so you're going to have less and less of that, and this tiny world where you don't even get to pick your friends. I don't know. That just seemed very sad at the time-- and scary, a little bit.

00:04:45.660 --> 00:04:56.050
<v Ira Glass>In fact, as senior year approached, as graduation day approached, Chris says that this tiny, two-minute speech by this teacher totally colored how he was seeing it. He loved his friends.

00:04:56.050 --> 00:05:22.020
<v Chris Benderev>I was scared of the end of high school in a way that I think most 18-year-olds aren't. It seemed like this was going to be the beginning of the end. And so I became very kind of nostalgic and also fearful, like a doomsday clock or something was running down.

00:05:22.020 --> 00:05:33.090
<v Ira Glass>Chris actually checked on the health teacher recently. And of course, he had no memory of making that speech, though he said it was exactly the kind of thing that he might have said. And in fact, he did remember saying it at some point to his own kids.

00:05:33.090 --> 00:05:52.950
<v Ira Glass>This teacher said that he would like to believe that he meant it in a kind of nice, "cherish these special times" sort of way, and he was horrified at the thought that this made Chris or any other kid feel bad for the rest of high school. But it just goes to show you how somebody can say something off the cuff that can accidentally turn somebody else's world completely upside down.

00:05:52.950 --> 00:06:02.380
<v Ira Glass>We asked listeners if they ever experienced this, and hundreds responded. Some of the sentences that were said casually to them that later, alone, they obsessed over.

00:06:02.380 --> 00:06:06.310
<v Ira Glass>"It's not your glasses that are uneven. It's your face."

00:06:06.310 --> 00:06:10.120
<v Ira Glass>"You must have been surrounded by some pretty insensitive people growing up."

00:06:10.120 --> 00:06:13.690
<v Ira Glass>"No, no, you're the only circumcised one in the family."

00:06:13.690 --> 00:06:22.810
<v Ira Glass>And one last one, said by a childhood acquaintance at a funeral. "Jenny, little Jenny, you're the one that nobody liked."

00:06:22.810 --> 00:06:27.340
<v Ira Glass>In Chris's case, the teacher's comment obviously stayed with him.

00:06:27.340 --> 00:06:29.500
<v Ira Glass>How old are you now?

00:06:29.500 --> 00:06:32.450
<v Chris Benderev>I am 38.

00:06:32.450 --> 00:06:35.800
<v Ira Glass>And how many friends from high school are you in touch with?

00:06:35.800 --> 00:06:39.490
<v Chris Benderev>I-- a few. A few.

00:06:39.490 --> 00:06:40.300
<v Ira Glass>Count.

00:06:40.300 --> 00:06:45.820
<v Chris Benderev>Three or four. Three or four. And really, only one that I see regularly.

00:06:45.820 --> 00:06:48.070
<v Ira Glass>So the guy was right.

00:06:48.070 --> 00:06:53.650
<v Chris Benderev>Yes, he was absolutely right. He-- 100%. [CHUCKLES] He fully predicted my future.

00:06:53.650 --> 00:06:56.650
<v Ira Glass>These days, Chris is married. One child.

00:06:56.650 --> 00:07:07.900
<v Chris Benderev>In fact, my-- the only friends I've made recently are the parents of the other kids who are in my son's daycare.

00:07:07.900 --> 00:07:16.570
<v Ira Glass>Yeah. And in fact, now that you are married and have children, is your life tedious and narrow and boring?

00:07:16.570 --> 00:07:20.810
<v Chris Benderev>In some ways, it is. But I like it. I really like it.

00:07:20.810 --> 00:07:23.200
<v Chris Benderev>[LAUGHTER]

00:07:23.200 --> 00:07:42.910
<v Chris Benderev>I obviously like spending time with my kid and my wife. And the people I made friends with that are the parents of the kids randomly assigned to my kid's daycare class-- yeah, they're delightful.

00:07:42.910 --> 00:07:55.550
<v Ira Glass>Today on our program, "If You Want to Destroy My Sweater, Hold This Thread As I Walk Away." We have stories about the things that people say that unravel your world, turn it upside down, shake it like a snow globe-- pick your own metaphor for this.

00:07:55.550 --> 00:08:29.090
<v Ira Glass>Some of these off-hand things that people say are completely accurate. Others are the exact opposite. And it can be really hard sometimes to tell which is which. We have real-life case examples, including somebody who thinks his life was completely upended after a single, brief, real-life encounter with Weezer. From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Stay with us.

00:08:29.090 --> 00:08:41.410
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. Act One, The World Has Turned and Left Me Here. So let's kick off this show about people saying things that unravel your picture of the world with this, from Lilly Sullivan.

00:08:41.410 --> 00:08:57.610
<v Lilly Sullivan>In my family, there's a story, the kind your family never forgets. It's about a hitchhiker. It happened decades ago, in 1974. There were three women in the car-- my aunt Manuelita, her daughter, and their cousin. Manuelita was driving.

00:08:57.610 --> 00:09:03.380
<v Manuelita>And I was going in my car. And then here, I saw him hitchhiking like this.

00:09:03.380 --> 00:09:04.680
<v Lilly Sullivan>Holding his finger in the air.

00:09:04.680 --> 00:09:05.530
<v Manuelita>Uh-huh.

00:09:05.530 --> 00:09:15.770
<v Lilly Sullivan>Manuelita is now 96, and her daughter in the car, Anita, remembers more of the details. So I'm going to let Anita tell a lot of this. She was a kid at the time, 10 years old.

00:09:15.770 --> 00:09:34.150
<v Anita>For me, it was such a shocking event. It permeated every cell of my being, meaning, like, I just remember it all very clearly. We were in the car. My mother was driving, and she was always impeccably dressed if she was going out.

00:09:34.150 --> 00:09:52.880
<v Anita>And, um, it was raining that day. My mother exited the freeway, and she spotted this young man hitchhiking. He was tall and lanky, and he had long, blonde, dirty blonde hair-- well, because it was wet. It was raining, you know.

00:09:52.880 --> 00:10:09.170
<v Anita>And his beard was just, like, down to his chest. And he had his shirt inside out and misbuttoned. And she said, oh, he has such kind eyes. And I was like, no, he doesn't. You can't see his eyes. It's raining.

00:10:09.170 --> 00:10:13.910
<v Manuelita>And she said, no! I was not afraid of him.

00:10:13.910 --> 00:10:15.560
<v Lilly Sullivan>But she was.

00:10:15.560 --> 00:10:17.900
<v Manuelita>Yeah. [LAUGHS]

00:10:17.900 --> 00:10:36.660
<v Lilly Sullivan>Anita was scared because they were in Northern California, and there were serial killers-- a few of them-- around there in the '70s. Even at 10, Anita knew this. All three women in the car were small, all under five feet. Anita in the front seat, their cousin Cecilia in the back. Again, here's Anita.

00:10:36.660 --> 00:10:45.900
<v Anita>And then, of course, I was protesting loudly. Don't stop the car! And Cecilia even said, no, Manuelita, don't do it. Don't do it.

00:10:45.900 --> 00:10:48.960
<v Lilly Sullivan>Manuelita stopped anyway. The man came to the window.

00:10:48.960 --> 00:10:57.450
<v Anita>And he had to talk through the passenger side window, where I was sitting and kind of, like, freaking out that he was in my face now.

00:10:57.450 --> 00:11:06.210
<v Lilly Sullivan>Anita remembers sitting stock still, staring straight ahead, afraid to make eye contact as a stranger somehow talked them into letting him into the car.

00:11:06.210 --> 00:11:14.180
<v Anita>And my mother didn't speak very good English. Neither did Cecelia. But guess who did speak Spanish?

00:11:14.180 --> 00:11:30.740
<v Lilly Sullivan>Serial killer-looking white guy? Turns out, fluent in Spanish. The hitchhiker lumbered into the back seat next to Cecilia. Cecilia had only been in the country a few weeks at that point and was like, what on earth? She was 26.

00:11:30.740 --> 00:11:47.030
<v Anita>And Cecilia-- she was all dressed prim and proper, and she even had little white gloves on. And he had a booming personality to match a booming voice. He had a great voice.

00:11:47.030 --> 00:11:59.570
<v Anita>He got in the car, and everybody calmed down when we heard him speak to Cecilia so kindly, and my mother introduced them. And she pointed out that she had just come.

00:11:59.570 --> 00:12:04.770
<v Lilly Sullivan>Cecilia had just come to the US from Peru. And this is how the story started.

00:12:04.770 --> 00:12:11.970
<v Anita>Their life changed. Our lives changed. It was like a meteor hitting the earth when we met Brian.

00:12:11.970 --> 00:12:32.110
<v Lilly Sullivan>Brian-- that was the hitchhiker's name, which I know because that guy's my dad. Cecilia-- that's my mom. And this story-- it's the story of how our family came to be, their legendary first meeting.

00:12:32.110 --> 00:12:51.320
<v Lilly Sullivan>It was followed by a similarly legendary first date. My mom's sister and cousin dressed her up in their own clothes-- white bell bottoms, white platform heels. My dad showed up in a poncho, and he took her hiking in the redwood forest, where it rained.

00:12:51.320 --> 00:13:12.050
<v Lilly Sullivan>He ended up carrying her so that she wouldn't ruin the shoes she'd borrowed. Two weeks later, they eloped, headed off to Reno, but ended up stopping in a random town nearby, where marriage licenses were $5 cheaper. They got married at 7:00 AM on Christmas Eve. Anita remembers them coming home after with their marriage license.

00:13:12.050 --> 00:13:24.140
<v Anita>I can remember even the knock on the door. And I ran to open it, and there they were, standing there. I yelled to my mom that they were here, and she came running.

00:13:24.140 --> 00:13:25.770
<v Lilly Sullivan>Her mom, Manuelita.

00:13:25.770 --> 00:13:33.420
<v Anita>She welcomed them in, and I don't know what happened next, but they came back married. And they loved each other until the end.

00:13:33.420 --> 00:13:37.830
<v Lilly Sullivan>Wow. Yeah. That's so cool. It's like, it's such a good story.

00:13:37.830 --> 00:13:51.430
<v Ira Glass>Like I said, legendary. This story is the bedrock foundation of how I see my parents, especially my dad. I picture him at 26, his misbuttoned shirt, catching rides through the West Coast alone.

00:13:51.430 --> 00:14:14.910
<v Ira Glass>This big white guy from Detroit climbing into this car full of immigrants, just exuberant and thinking, wow, I'm going to marry this lady. And a picture of my mom at 26, having just arrived in the country, self-contained, determined, seeing this weirdo and deciding, yes. Him.

00:14:14.910 --> 00:14:23.290
<v Ira Glass>When you enter the family, this is pretty much the one story we make you memorize. And then you could be a citizen of the family. Here's my brother-in-law, Lars.

00:14:23.290 --> 00:14:25.150
<v Lilly Sullivan>How many times do you think you've heard this story?

00:14:25.150 --> 00:14:30.540
<v Lars>Oh, I don't know. Many times. 50, 100. [CHUCKLES] Many times.

00:14:30.540 --> 00:14:33.910
<v Lilly Sullivan>And the meaning of this story has always been clear.

00:14:33.910 --> 00:14:39.240
<v Karl>If they didn't pick him up, we would-- all of us wouldn't be here right now.

00:14:39.240 --> 00:14:41.050
<v Lilly Sullivan>Here I am with my niece and nephews.

00:14:41.050 --> 00:14:45.810
<v Lilly Sullivan>I wouldn't be here, and you wouldn't be here. Your mom wouldn't be here.

00:14:45.810 --> 00:14:47.980
<v Karl>And Sammy wouldn't be here.

00:14:47.980 --> 00:14:49.240
<v Lilly Sullivan>Sammy wouldn't be here.

00:14:49.240 --> 00:14:53.490
<v Karl>Tia Nena wouldn't be here. Marian wouldn't be here. Wally wouldn't be here.

00:14:53.490 --> 00:14:55.690
<v Sammy>Anyone that we know wouldn't be here.

00:14:55.690 --> 00:14:56.430
<v Cece>Wait, no!

00:14:56.430 --> 00:14:58.370
<v Lilly Sullivan>Well, we wouldn't be here to know them.

00:14:58.370 --> 00:14:58.720
<v Lilly Sullivan>[KARL LAUGHS]

00:14:58.720 --> 00:15:04.920
<v Lilly Sullivan>[LAUGHTER]

00:15:04.920 --> 00:15:14.220
<v Ira Glass>There's something predestined about it. This is such an important story, I thought. You know what? I want to visit the spot where the meteor struck.

00:15:14.220 --> 00:15:25.740
<v Ira Glass>My dad died 10 years ago. I miss him, always. And he doesn't have a grave. He insisted on cremation by, quote, "the cheapest means possible."

00:15:25.740 --> 00:15:51.150
<v Ira Glass>He didn't like fancy things. And I also think that he didn't want to be a burden. Anyway, what I want to remember him was not, like, a location I can go to. I can't, like, put flowers by a tombstone. So how about this place? This legendary spot where he climbed into a car and our family began. So, can't be hard. So where was it?

00:15:51.150 --> 00:16:10.410
<v Ira Glass>Anita says it was by the freeway exit by our house. Manuelita says it was an onramp heading downtown. But they're not definitive about it. So I went to the third person in the car that day, my mom. And she says, sure, I know exactly where it was. And then she starts to tell me this story.

00:16:10.410 --> 00:16:18.700
<v Cecilia>I remember. What I remember is that we were walking down the street, walking to the car. And Brian said, hola.

00:16:18.700 --> 00:16:20.580
<v Lilly Sullivan>Brian, my dad.

00:16:20.580 --> 00:16:23.310
<v Cecilia>And Manuelita said, hola, hola. We all say, hola.

00:16:23.310 --> 00:16:25.600
<v Lilly Sullivan>You saw him when he was walking?

00:16:25.600 --> 00:16:31.230
<v Cecilia>Yes. He was walking to go to the bus stop.

00:16:31.230 --> 00:16:42.610
<v Lilly Sullivan>The bus stop? He was taking the bus? This is not the story I'd always heard. In my mom's version, they weren't in a car. She and Manuelita were walking down the street.

00:16:42.610 --> 00:16:56.520
<v Lilly Sullivan>They had just left the Jacksons' house. The Jacksons were a family where my tia Manuelita worked as a cook. She says it was a beautiful day, not raining at all. And most importantly, dad wasn't hitchhiking.

00:16:56.520 --> 00:16:59.770
<v Lilly Sullivan>Was he, like, holding up a sign or something, saying he wanted a ride?

00:16:59.770 --> 00:17:01.280
<v Cecilia>No.

00:17:01.280 --> 00:17:03.750
<v Lilly Sullivan>Because the story has always been hitchhiking.

00:17:03.750 --> 00:17:07.530
<v Cecilia>No.

00:17:07.530 --> 00:17:14.620
<v Lilly Sullivan>Wait, Mom, but your story and Anita's story is completely different. She remembers it clearly.

00:17:14.620 --> 00:17:21.450
<v Cecilia>Your dad was walking to the stop.

00:17:21.450 --> 00:17:25.830
<v Lilly Sullivan>But then why does she remember this other story?

00:17:25.830 --> 00:17:27.600
<v Cecilia>I don't know.

00:17:27.600 --> 00:17:40.050
<v Lilly Sullivan>From my mom's point of view, this is especially mysterious because she's quite certain that Anita wasn't there. Not in a car, not on the street. Not there for this moment at all.

00:17:40.050 --> 00:17:42.080
<v Cecilia>I don't think Anita was.

00:17:42.080 --> 00:17:43.910
<v Lilly Sullivan>Anita remembers it.

00:17:43.910 --> 00:17:46.860
<v Cecilia>I don't think she was. No. No, no.

00:17:46.860 --> 00:17:47.510
<v Lilly Sullivan>OK, OK.

00:17:47.510 --> 00:18:03.710
<v Ira Glass>This kind of knocked me over. The hitchhiking story, as I've said, is the origin story of my family. My mom's had a private version of it for 50 years that she's kept herself during the many, many conversations where we tell it?

00:18:03.710 --> 00:18:16.550
<v Ira Glass>When my dad died 10 years ago, we wrote about this story in his obituary. Like, printed it in our local newspaper. We ran that obituary by my mom. She didn't think it was worth correcting?

00:18:16.550 --> 00:18:32.930
<v Ira Glass>Dad was the memory keeper of our family, a big-hearted, big-brained guy who held on to everything that happened. Who had chicken pox first as a kid? Natalie, he'd say. What was the name of that iguana that we had that died? Mari-iguana, he'd say.

00:18:32.930 --> 00:18:48.140
<v Ira Glass>He would absolutely know exactly where this happened. And the thought that he's not here to tell us-- it makes him feel so gone, like we had a favorite photo of him, and we have no idea where it is anymore.

00:18:48.140 --> 00:19:03.080
<v Ira Glass>When my dad died, it was sudden, and it devastated me. As time passes, we've lost so much of him. His clothes have lost his smell of wool and sawdust and too much Tide laundry detergent.

00:19:03.080 --> 00:19:27.800
<v Ira Glass>And this, it was like losing a big piece of him again, because in his absence and in our negligence, we simply forgot to remember. Unforgivable. I had to fix this. I had to get to the truth.

00:19:27.800 --> 00:19:43.390
<v Ira Glass>I forced the three of them-- Manuelita, Anita, and my mom-- to sit down together to try to work this out, come to some agreement about what happened and where. Anita is stunned to hear that my mom and Manuelita don't think she was in the car.

00:19:43.390 --> 00:19:50.120
<v Anita>So yeah, my story is not going to change. I was in the car. I was in the front seat. You were in the back seat.

00:19:50.120 --> 00:19:51.670
<v Lilly Sullivan>Mom turns to me.

00:19:51.670 --> 00:19:54.400
<v Cecilia>I don't remember never Anita being around.

00:19:54.400 --> 00:19:58.300
<v Lilly Sullivan>She tells the others they weren't even in a car.

00:19:58.300 --> 00:20:03.310
<v Lilly Sullivan>But Manuelita, you remember him hitchhiking, and you were driving, and you pulled over, right?

00:20:03.310 --> 00:20:07.910
<v Manuelita>Yeah. Of course. That's why I picked him up.

00:20:07.910 --> 00:20:08.410
<v Lilly Sullivan>Yeah.

00:20:08.410 --> 00:20:09.070
<v Manuelita>[LAUGHS]

00:20:09.070 --> 00:20:13.030
<v Lilly Sullivan>She remembers walking, that you all were walking.

00:20:13.030 --> 00:20:21.260
<v Manuelita>No, we were not walking. Oh, yeah, I was driving. Yeah, I was driving. Yeah, hitchhiking. He was hitchhiking.

00:20:21.260 --> 00:20:38.090
<v Lilly Sullivan>This went nowhere. And the fact that we've been telling this hitchhiking story for 50 years and my mom's never mentioned that she thinks it's complete bullshit? I have to say, that's very much like my mom. She's eminently capable of keeping her thoughts and feelings to herself.

00:20:38.090 --> 00:20:40.280
<v Lilly Sullivan>She has feelings, obviously.

00:20:40.280 --> 00:21:03.850
<v Lilly Sullivan>But she shows love in concrete ways. An unasked-for plate of fruit, a bowl of soup. She'd give me her kidney or hide a body for me, no questions asked. But sitting around gabbing about feelings-- not her thing. She finds that trying. She'll either roll her eyes or blurt out something explosive and walk away or clam up. Here's us in the car.

00:21:03.850 --> 00:21:11.450
<v Lilly Sullivan>Mom, so, but-- but-- I just-- is it interesting to you that you have one memory and other people have a different memory? Is it interesting?

00:21:11.450 --> 00:21:18.910
<v Cecilia>Um, I know that stories are like that.

00:21:18.910 --> 00:21:22.880
<v Lilly Sullivan>I know, I know, but is it interesting? I want to talk about the feelings of it.

00:21:22.880 --> 00:21:27.250
<v Cecilia>That's good. That's how we met.

00:21:27.250 --> 00:21:29.930
<v Lilly Sullivan>Yeah, but what's it-- what's it like? How do you feel?

00:21:29.930 --> 00:21:33.820
<v Cecilia>Nothing. It's OK. [VEHICLE HONKING] Ay, ay, ay.

00:21:33.820 --> 00:21:44.330
<v Lilly Sullivan>She gets impatient. She dodges. In response, I get impatient with her, about everything. I compulsively nitpick everything she does.

00:21:44.330 --> 00:21:47.280
<v Lilly Sullivan>Can you put your bag in back? Because of the noise. It's too much noise.

00:21:47.280 --> 00:21:47.990
<v Cecilia>Yeah.

00:21:47.990 --> 00:21:53.640
<v Lilly Sullivan>Rustling that bag makes noise that gets on the mic, I tell her. So does her beaded necklace.

00:21:53.640 --> 00:21:55.140
<v Lilly Sullivan>Could you take off your necklace?

00:21:55.140 --> 00:21:59.840
<v Cecilia>Yeah. [SPEAKS SPANISH]

00:21:59.840 --> 00:22:06.870
<v Lilly Sullivan>Rather than engage with me, she whips out her little pot of Mary Kay cold cream and starts stabbing it on her cheeks and forehead.

00:22:06.870 --> 00:22:11.330
<v Cecilia>Put some cream on my face. [SPEAKS SPANISH]

00:22:11.330 --> 00:22:12.740
<v Lilly Sullivan>Mom, let's focus.

00:22:12.740 --> 00:22:15.440
<v Cecilia>OK. I'm not going to-- OK.

00:22:15.440 --> 00:22:17.280
<v Lilly Sullivan>Can you just put the cream on after we go?

00:22:17.280 --> 00:22:18.140
<v Cecilia>OK.

00:22:18.140 --> 00:22:18.830
<v Lilly Sullivan>But no--

00:22:18.830 --> 00:22:19.830
<v Cecilia>It's OK. Let's go now.

00:22:19.830 --> 00:22:21.600
<v Lilly Sullivan>You can't be messing with it as we drive.

00:22:21.600 --> 00:22:23.150
<v Cecilia>OK.

00:22:23.150 --> 00:22:48.200
<v Lilly Sullivan>I'm a nightmare. She lets it go. She's a good mom. Of course, the day my parents met, there was one other person there-- my dad. I'd interviewed him in 2010, years before he died, before he even got sick.

00:22:48.200 --> 00:23:03.350
<v Lilly Sullivan>I've never been able to bring myself to listen to that recording. Just too hard. So I had no memory of what we'd talked about that day. But I had a hunch that if I'd done an interview with him, I would have asked him to tell me this story.

00:23:03.350 --> 00:23:22.700
<v Lilly Sullivan>I had no idea where this interview was, but I'd given him a copy, and I knew he would have kept it. The week I talked to my mom, I spent hours digging through old file cabinets and boxes in the garage. I finally found it one night at 2:00 AM. I threw on all the lights, ran into bed, and listened immediately.

00:23:22.700 --> 00:23:27.110
<v Lilly Sullivan>OK. Um, how did you meet Mom?

00:23:27.110 --> 00:23:35.840
<v Brian>I was hitchhiking on Rio Del Mar Boulevard. And Manuelita picked me up, and I believe Anita was in the car, too.

00:23:35.840 --> 00:23:38.670
<v Lilly Sullivan>Oh, my god. Of course he has all the answers.

00:23:38.670 --> 00:23:49.320
<v Brian>Anita said, no, don't-- don't even think of stopping for this guy. And Manuelita said, he's cute! And, uh-- and that's how I met your mom.

00:23:49.320 --> 00:23:50.970
<v Lilly Sullivan>What kind of car were they driving?

00:23:50.970 --> 00:24:10.110
<v Brian>Huge. A Lincoln Continental. [CHUCKLES] Yeah, it was-- I mean, it was like a Star Wars machine, you know. The front of it went by, and then five minutes later, the rear of it went by. It was huge. Biggest car in the world. Manuelita, the smallest person in the world, is driving it.

00:24:10.110 --> 00:24:13.000
<v Brian>[LAUGHTER]

00:24:13.000 --> 00:24:18.020
<v Lilly Sullivan>Um, and what did you think when you got in the car?

00:24:18.020 --> 00:24:21.350
<v Brian>I said, um, she's a cutie.

00:24:21.350 --> 00:24:23.570
<v Lilly Sullivan>That's the first thing you thought when you got in the car?

00:24:23.570 --> 00:24:24.330
<v Brian>Of course. Yeah, yeah.

00:24:24.330 --> 00:24:29.660
<v Lilly Sullivan>You weren't like, who are these tiny ladies picking up a huge-- [LAUGHS] like-- [LAUGHS]

00:24:29.660 --> 00:24:35.740
<v Brian>I-- I didn't know. I didn't know what I was getting into.

00:24:35.740 --> 00:24:37.710
<v Lilly Sullivan>Oh. Why did you think she was cute?

00:24:37.710 --> 00:24:41.980
<v Brian>She was very self-confident and--

00:24:41.980 --> 00:24:43.380
<v Lilly Sullivan>Oh, really?

00:24:43.380 --> 00:24:59.730
<v Brian>Well, you know your mom. She's nothing if not self-confident. Yeah. She's smart. Yeah, she's really smart. Really smart.

00:24:59.730 --> 00:25:04.170
<v Lilly Sullivan>So they picked you up on Rio Del Mar. In front of what? Like, what would be there today?

00:25:04.170 --> 00:25:12.870
<v Brian>Same. It hasn't changed. Rio Del Mar. And right by that bridge-- you know the bridge on Rio Del Mar Boulevard?

00:25:12.870 --> 00:25:13.610
<v Lilly Sullivan>The one by, like--

00:25:13.610 --> 00:25:15.970
<v Brian>The little bridge that goes across that little ravine there.

00:25:15.970 --> 00:25:16.780
<v Lilly Sullivan>Oh, right there?

00:25:16.780 --> 00:25:18.480
<v Brian>Yeah, right there. Uh-huh.

00:25:18.480 --> 00:25:21.930
<v Lilly Sullivan>And so then what happened in the car? When did you get out?

00:25:21.930 --> 00:25:27.400
<v Brian>They invited me over to dinner. Manuelita did. So I went over to dinner.

00:25:27.400 --> 00:25:29.140
<v Lilly Sullivan>Anita was against this.

00:25:29.140 --> 00:25:30.720
<v Brian>Very much against this.

00:25:30.720 --> 00:25:34.740
<v Lilly Sullivan>And then did you ask Mom out?

00:25:34.740 --> 00:25:43.410
<v Brian>Either that or Manuelita asked us both-- I think Manuelita asked us both out. She said, when are you-- when are you coming to take her out? Something like that.

00:25:43.410 --> 00:25:46.260
<v Brian>[LAUGHTER]

00:25:46.260 --> 00:25:47.470
<v Lilly Sullivan>What did you say?

00:25:47.470 --> 00:25:51.690
<v Brian>I said, oh, I don't know. Tomorrow? She goes, OK.

00:25:51.690 --> 00:26:08.970
<v Lilly Sullivan>This recording is from 14 years ago. I haven't really heard his voice in 10 years, since he died. I hadn't forgotten, but I sort of had forgotten, how much fun we had just talking to each other.

00:26:08.970 --> 00:26:24.000
<v Lilly Sullivan>And my dad told the same story as Anita and Manuelita. This story my dad tells about their meeting was not news to my mom. She says, yeah, we always disagreed about that. Of course he said that. He's got it wrong. Always has.

00:26:24.000 --> 00:26:35.670
<v Cecilia>I remember we argued a lot when we told people how we met. People laughed because he wants to say one thing, I want to say another thing.

00:26:35.670 --> 00:26:51.450
<v Lilly Sullivan>I thought a lot about why my mom prefers her version, where he's walking to a bus stop and not hitchhiking. And the main thing I keep thinking about is, in my dad's version, my mom's people make the first move. Their meeting is kind of random, a split-second fluke.

00:26:51.450 --> 00:27:20.550
<v Lilly Sullivan>But in the version my mom likes, everyone's on foot, on this rainless, beautiful day, and my dad sees them and approaches. He makes the first move, which is maybe more romantic. Everyone wants to be chosen. I run my hypothesis by her. She kind of blinks at me blankly, slightly impatient. Nothing.

00:27:20.550 --> 00:27:28.740
<v Lilly Sullivan>The day after I found that interview with my dad, I woke up early. And the mismatched memories-- it all started clicking together.

00:27:28.740 --> 00:27:44.830
<v Lilly Sullivan>OK, this is just me in my room. It's Wednesday. [CLEARS THROAT] Last night, I listened to that recording, and Dad said it was in Rio Del Mar, right by the bridge. So I think-- I think I just figured it out.

00:27:44.830 --> 00:28:00.510
<v Ira Glass>I think their car was parked on the street a little ways from the Jacksons' house, and they had to walk to the car from the house. So my mom remembers that walk. And then they got in and had just started driving when they hit that bridge and saw my dad.

00:28:00.510 --> 00:28:15.750
<v Ira Glass>That's, like, a block away from the Jacksons'. No time at all. Easy for my mom to forget. And there he stood, not at a bus stop, but hitchhiking. OK, here's me explaining my theory to my mom.

00:28:15.750 --> 00:28:20.740
<v Lilly Sullivan>And you had barely gotten in the car. You went around the corner, and Dad was right there.

00:28:20.740 --> 00:28:23.590
<v Cecilia>Mm-hmm. We drove around the corner?

00:28:23.590 --> 00:28:26.740
<v Lilly Sullivan>Maybe. Because what Dad said-- and Dad has a really good memory, you know.

00:28:26.740 --> 00:28:28.540
<v Cecilia>Oh, yeah, I know. [LAUGHS]

00:28:28.540 --> 00:28:31.700
<v Lilly Sullivan>But what I think might have happened is-- Mom, your feet. Can you stop?

00:28:31.700 --> 00:28:32.080
<v Cecilia>Yeah.

00:28:32.080 --> 00:28:37.220
<v Lilly Sullivan>I think you guys might have just gotten in the car, barely driven, and then he was right there.

00:28:37.220 --> 00:28:40.750
<v Cecilia>Yeah, I think so. I think that's what it is.

00:28:40.750 --> 00:28:42.420
<v Lilly Sullivan>Do you think that sounds right?

00:28:42.420 --> 00:28:49.600
<v Cecilia>Mm-hmm. I think it sounds right. Yeah, that's how I remember. Not much driving.

00:28:49.600 --> 00:28:53.180
<v Lilly Sullivan>Yeah. Do you want to go drive and see where he said? Let's go look at--

00:28:53.180 --> 00:29:00.880
<v Cecilia>Sure. [SPEAKS SPANISH] We are close by. Yeah.

00:29:00.880 --> 00:29:03.980
<v Lilly Sullivan>We drive to the spot my dad said. You remember.

00:29:03.980 --> 00:29:09.700
<v Brian>Rio Del Mar and right by that bridge. The little bridge that goes across that little ravine.

00:29:09.700 --> 00:29:19.780
<v Lilly Sullivan>The Jackson house is maybe a minute away, around the corner. There are trees everywhere, an intersection between residential blocks. Not much around, except for--

00:29:19.780 --> 00:29:24.260
<v Cecilia>This bus stop. He was walking here.

00:29:24.260 --> 00:29:25.850
<v Lilly Sullivan>Mom, it's a bus stop!

00:29:25.850 --> 00:29:26.350
<v Cecilia>Yeah.

00:29:26.350 --> 00:29:30.040
<v Cecilia>[LAUGHTER]

00:29:30.040 --> 00:29:32.510
<v Lilly Sullivan>I've never seen this bus stop before.

00:29:32.510 --> 00:29:36.980
<v Ira Glass>She had been talking about this bus stop the whole time, and I didn't believe her.

00:29:36.980 --> 00:29:41.830
<v Ira Glass>[LAUGHTER]

00:29:41.830 --> 00:29:44.990
<v Lilly Sullivan>Mom, it's the bus stop! The one you've been talking about.

00:29:44.990 --> 00:29:49.780
<v Cecilia>Yeah. But he was walking to the bus stop.

00:29:49.780 --> 00:29:52.780
<v Lilly Sullivan>I think this is it, Mom.

00:29:52.780 --> 00:29:54.490
<v Cecilia>Oh, I'm so glad. Yeah.

00:29:54.490 --> 00:30:05.710
<v Lilly Sullivan>I knew this road like the back of my hand, and I'd never seen a bus stop there. But here it was, tucked under some trees, just a sign and a little bench.

00:30:05.710 --> 00:30:07.990
<v Lilly Sullivan>Mom, good job. [LAUGHS]

00:30:07.990 --> 00:30:11.950
<v Cecilia>Well, the past memory never goes away, they say.

00:30:11.950 --> 00:30:12.770
<v Lilly Sullivan>I'm so relieved.

00:30:12.770 --> 00:30:23.660
<v Cecilia>[LAUGHS] Yeah. Very close. Everything is so close. We found the bus stop.

00:30:23.660 --> 00:30:26.020
<v Lilly Sullivan>[SIGHS] Wow.

00:30:26.020 --> 00:30:41.020
<v Ira Glass>This was it, what I'd wanted to find, the place our family began, a bus stop I'd driven past a million times. Not the fanciest spot in the world, but pretty, a place you wouldn't mind visiting again.

00:30:41.020 --> 00:31:02.370
<v Ira Glass>My mom said next time I'm in town, we should go sit at that bus stop, bring champagne, toast my dad. Probably get a ticket, she said. But to hell with it.

00:31:02.370 --> 00:31:17.860
<v Ira Glass>Part of what made this whole project a little weird for me was this thing that I've mentioned a few times, that my mom doesn't really like discussing feelings. But I learned something, talking to my sister Kim about all this stuff, that driving home, I really wanted to tell my mom.

00:31:17.860 --> 00:31:23.700
<v Lilly Sullivan>Do you know that when he was sick, Manuelita came to the house and she was sitting with him.

00:31:23.700 --> 00:31:23.950
<v Cecilia>Mm-hmm.

00:31:23.950 --> 00:31:35.960
<v Lilly Sullivan>And, um-- and he was sick, you know. He was just lying down and not really talking that much at that point. But he did say to her, he said, Manuelita, thank you for my life.

00:31:35.960 --> 00:31:39.280
<v Cecilia>Hmm.

00:31:39.280 --> 00:31:40.010
<v Lilly Sullivan>Did you know that?

00:31:40.010 --> 00:31:44.780
<v Cecilia>He said-- I don't remember. I think he said something, yeah.

00:31:44.780 --> 00:31:50.830
<v Lilly Sullivan>What, um-- what does it feel like to hear that he said that?

00:31:50.830 --> 00:31:52.450
<v Cecilia>It's nice.

00:31:52.450 --> 00:31:54.890
<v Lilly Sullivan>Tell me more. Tell me more about what it feels like.

00:31:54.890 --> 00:31:57.940
<v Cecilia>What I feel like-- I feel like crying.

00:31:57.940 --> 00:31:59.090
<v Lilly Sullivan>What?

00:31:59.090 --> 00:32:04.090
<v Cecilia>(CRYING) Like crying.

00:32:04.090 --> 00:32:05.470
<v Lilly Sullivan>You feel like crying?

00:32:05.470 --> 00:32:07.630
<v Cecilia>Yeah.

00:32:07.630 --> 00:32:13.180
<v Cecilia>[CRYING]

00:32:13.180 --> 00:32:23.060
<v Cecilia>If I had known that he was going to die so fast, so soon.

00:32:23.060 --> 00:32:33.390
<v Lilly Sullivan>Well, I think, when I hear that story, it's kind of beautiful to me, because he loved his life so much.

00:32:33.390 --> 00:32:34.310
<v Cecilia>He loved it, yeah.

00:32:34.310 --> 00:32:37.020
<v Lilly Sullivan>And he loved his family, and he loved you.

00:32:37.020 --> 00:32:42.080
<v Cecilia>Mm, yeah. Yeah.

00:32:42.080 --> 00:32:42.960
<v Lilly Sullivan>Right?

00:32:42.960 --> 00:32:56.870
<v Cecilia>Mm-hmm. He said thanks to me, too. And you know, we're not perfect, but we can forgive if we have hurt-- if we-- when he was dying.

00:32:56.870 --> 00:33:13.950
<v Lilly Sullivan>I brought up my dad's last days, and my mom's mind went straight to their last night together. Cut to the heart of her grief, to this moment when he was dying and they forgave each other for their hurts. We've never talked about this. My mom never talks like this.

00:33:13.950 --> 00:33:29.210
<v Cecilia>You know, he said, the number one thing-- that everything was in order, and I forgive him, whatever he did. Because we're not perfect, and he forgave me, too, whatever I did. So we talked in the room, you know, at the end?

00:33:29.210 --> 00:33:30.830
<v Lilly Sullivan>Yeah.

00:33:30.830 --> 00:33:55.310
<v Cecilia>And then we hugged, and I'm so glad we sleep together the last night. But I was so afraid to touch him. I didn't want to hurt him. So I just touched his legs and his feet, and he slept the whole night. No problem.

00:33:55.310 --> 00:34:04.970
<v Lilly Sullivan>Mom didn't stop there. She told me something else. When I was nine, my parents hit a rough patch in their relationship and decided to separate.

00:34:04.970 --> 00:34:17.480
<v Lilly Sullivan>After a few months, they got back together. I never really knew how or why. We didn't like to speak about that time in my family. But as I was talking to my mom about all this, she brought it up.

00:34:17.480 --> 00:34:35.840
<v Lilly Sullivan>You want to know the real hitchhiking story, she said. And she told me that during the time they were separated, one day, she was driving down the street, and she saw my dad was walking. And as she approached him on the road, he saw it was her in her Volvo, and he threw his thumb in the air. Cool joke, huh?

00:34:35.840 --> 00:34:50.060
<v Lilly Sullivan>So she stopped, picked him up. A couple of days later, they got back together. That's the important story, she said. After that, my parents stayed married another 20 years.

00:34:50.060 --> 00:35:16.370
<v Lilly Sullivan>During that time, they had a blast together, sometimes inseparable. The best time of their marriage, my mom tells me, after they returned to that root moment where everything started. Only this time, it wasn't random chance. It was his choice to flag her down and hers to scoop him up.

00:35:16.370 --> 00:35:25.520
<v Ira Glass>Lilly Sullivan is a producer on our show. Special thanks to Lilly's sisters. This song is one of their dad's favorites. He used to play it for them on the piano when they were growing up.

00:35:25.520 --> 00:35:53.960
<v Ira Glass>["YOU AIN'T GOIN' NOWHERE" BY BOB DYLAN]

00:35:53.960 --> 00:36:06.660
<v Ira Glass>Coming up, one badly tuned instrument on one song at one concert can change your life. That's in a minute, on Chicago Public Radio, when our program continues.

00:36:06.660 --> 00:36:23.040
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Today's program-- "If You Want to Destroy My Sweater, Hold This Thread As I Walk Away." We have stories about small moments between people that suddenly change how everything looks. We've arrived at Act Two of our program. Act Two, What's With These Homies Dissing My Girl?

00:36:23.040 --> 00:36:36.990
<v Ira Glass>In his early 20s, Mike Comite wanted to be a professional musician. He was trying his absolute hardest to make that happen, until one day, it all came undone-- weirdly, right in front of Weezer. Mike tells what happened.

00:36:36.990 --> 00:36:57.060
<v Mike Comite>There's this video. I've watched it more times than I'd like to admit in the last decade and a half, kind of like a retired football player watching old game tapes from his glory days, who pauses and rewinds that one play where he tore his ACL over and over to see where his career went sideways. It happened at Bonnaroo, this music festival with some of the biggest acts in the world.

00:36:57.060 --> 00:37:11.520
<v Mike Comite>That year, Dave Matthews Band and Jay-Z were playing. It was 2010. The band I was in was invited to play on one of the medium-sized stages, which was a huge deal for us. There'd be over 2,000 people watching. It'd be the biggest stage we'd ever played on.

00:37:11.520 --> 00:37:23.070
<v Mike Comite>But then, I saw that Weezer was also playing Bonnaroo. It felt like some kind of fated opportunity. We were actually covering a Weezer song in our set, and so I had this big idea.

00:37:23.070 --> 00:37:41.880
<v Mike Comite>Our front person, Julia, was a musician who had blown up on YouTube. She had thousands of followers on social media. What if we got them all to tweet at Weezer's lead singer, Rivers Cuomo, to see if he'd come sing with us? I asked Julia, and she was game for it, and shockingly, it worked, better than we could have hoped.

00:37:41.880 --> 00:38:01.780
<v Mike Comite>Rivers didn't come sing with us. Instead, he invited Julia to come play her ukulele and sing with Weezer during one of their songs on the main stage. Julia's visibility at Bonnaroo would be multiplied tenfold. If this went well, who knows where it could lead? Maybe Weezer would bring us on tour with them as their opener.

00:38:01.780 --> 00:38:12.570
<v Mike Comite>Our agent immediately submitted us to their team for some of their upcoming shows. Or maybe Rivers would write a song with Julia, play on our record, or she'd play on theirs. The possibilities kept me up at night.

00:38:12.570 --> 00:38:36.900
<v Mike Comite>I was 22. I'd only been playing music professionally for a year at that point. But I was convinced this would be the moment that would transform our careers. The day of the show, Julia split off from our group to rehearse with Weezer on their tour bus. I felt a little sting finding out I wasn't going to meet them, too.

00:38:36.900 --> 00:38:48.810
<v Mike Comite>Weezer went on a little before sunset. Me and the rest of the band sat way back in the crowd on some bleachers, while Julia was somewhere backstage. There had to be at least 20,000 people between us and Weezer.

00:38:48.810 --> 00:38:59.170
<v Mike Comite>I couldn't believe that a dumb idea I'd cooked up in rehearsal had led to this. The song Julia was going to play on was called "Tripping Down the Freeway." Weezer started it a few songs into their set.

00:38:59.170 --> 00:39:02.730
<v Rivers Cuomo>Here's a new song called, "Tripping Down the Freeway."

00:39:02.730 --> 00:39:06.000
<v Rivers Cuomo>[CROWD CHEERING]

00:39:06.000 --> 00:39:14.770
<v Mike Comite>Julia is off stage for the first half of the song. But after the guitar solo, Rivers brings the band down to a vamp and has Julia come out kind of dramatically.

00:39:14.770 --> 00:39:27.180
<v Rivers Cuomo>All right. We're going to bring out a special guest now! Bonnaroo, this is Julia Nunes on ukulele.

00:39:27.180 --> 00:39:31.090
<v Mike Comite>Julia walks on stage, strumming her uke, but something sounds off.

00:39:31.090 --> 00:39:32.760
<v Rivers Cuomo>Are we in the right tuning?

00:39:32.760 --> 00:39:36.010
<v Mike Comite>They are not in the right tuning. She's in a different key.

00:39:36.010 --> 00:39:37.620
<v Rivers Cuomo>Oh, it's supposed to be E-flat.

00:39:37.620 --> 00:39:42.960
<v Mike Comite>For a split second, I'm convinced I'm having a nightmare. I try telling myself to wake up.

00:39:42.960 --> 00:39:46.620
<v Scott Shriner>She's got this amazing tuner on her ukulele. We'll get it straightened out right now.

00:39:46.620 --> 00:39:55.080
<v Mike Comite>Nope. Weezer is still on stage, having a conversation mid-song about Julia's tuning through the PA system. Rivers pivots.

00:39:55.080 --> 00:39:58.570
<v Rivers Cuomo>All right. I'm going to tell a little story while you tune your ukulele.

00:39:58.570 --> 00:40:05.530
<v Mike Comite>He turns to the crowd and starts talking about how Julia had ended up on stage with him that day, about Twitter, about Julia's fans.

00:40:05.530 --> 00:40:20.440
<v Rivers Cuomo>They started to hit me up, saying, hey, dawwwg, Julia's an amazing singer and ukuleleist. Most of the time, she even knows what key the song is in, so why don't you guys have her up?

00:40:20.440 --> 00:40:24.710
<v Mike Comite>He's making fun of her? I start clenching my jaw from the stands.

00:40:24.710 --> 00:40:40.600
<v Rivers Cuomo>I said, I don't know, man! This is Weezer. This is a professional act. We demand perfection. And Julia's fans said, trust us, dawg. Julia's the bomb!

00:40:40.600 --> 00:40:58.090
<v Mike Comite>I'm mortified. I can't believe how mean he's being to her. Just so passive-aggressive. At this point, our crew member who's been sitting next to me, excitedly filming Julia's big moment, quietly stops recording and puts down her camera. This isn't her video. Someone else posted this one on YouTube.

00:40:58.090 --> 00:41:11.780
<v Mike Comite>I couldn't help thinking, I'd been the one to suggest she do this in the first place. This was on me. After an excruciating 62 seconds of improv, Rivers wanders across the stage to where Julia is trying to retune with another band member.

00:41:11.780 --> 00:41:16.020
<v Rivers Cuomo>So, uh, we could just have you sing.

00:41:16.020 --> 00:41:17.270
<v Julia Nunes>I'm just going to sing.

00:41:17.270 --> 00:41:19.020
<v Mike Comite>I'm just going to sing, she says.

00:41:19.020 --> 00:41:22.490
<v Rivers Cuomo>All right. She's just going to sing. Check your mic.

00:41:22.490 --> 00:41:23.520
<v Man>Oh, what the hell.

00:41:23.520 --> 00:41:24.140
<v Julia Nunes>[YELLS]

00:41:24.140 --> 00:41:25.100
<v Rivers Cuomo>Yeah!

00:41:25.100 --> 00:41:26.800
<v Rivers Cuomo>[CROWD CHEERING]

00:41:26.800 --> 00:41:28.070
<v Julia Nunes>Yeah!

00:41:28.070 --> 00:41:29.990
<v Rivers Cuomo>All right, give us the bass line, Scott.

00:41:29.990 --> 00:41:32.900
<v Mike Comite>Rivers counts off and starts singing, and Julia jumps in.

00:41:32.900 --> 00:41:41.070
<v Rivers Cuomo>1, 2, 3. (SINGING) No way. We ain't going to break up. We made a promise, and our will won't fade out.

00:41:41.070 --> 00:41:59.600
<v Mike Comite>Julia looks so small from where we're sitting. But I can tell she's holding her ukulele by her side as she sings. They finish the song. Rivers thanks Julia, and she walks off the stage. I'm sitting in the bleachers, shocked by what just happened. Weezer keeps playing a bunch of my favorite songs, and I can't enjoy them.

00:41:59.600 --> 00:42:04.340
<v Rivers Cuomo>Give me the guitar, my man.

00:42:04.340 --> 00:42:23.380
<v Mike Comite>The next day, Rivers tweeted at Julia and thanked her for playing despite the mishap. A few weeks later, Weezer's manager officially turned us down for the opening spot on tour.

00:42:23.380 --> 00:42:35.080
<v Mike Comite>In the music business, you need some moment to pull you out of obscurity and propel you forward. And it felt like, for us, Weezer had been it. Instead, we'd blown it. And that was the beginning of the end.

00:42:35.080 --> 00:42:54.130
<v Mike Comite>After Bonnaroo, Julia and I stayed busy touring, just the two of us, for a while. But then things slowed down. Julia and her management were about to part ways. I started getting nervous about being able to support myself, so I tapped out of touring with Julia and got a full-time job where a lot of struggling musicians and actors end up-- at the Apple Store.

00:42:54.130 --> 00:43:15.350
<v Mike Comite>And so this is when I started watching and rewatching that video, the Weezer performance. I've been doing that for the last 14 years. Each replay, I keep hoping that it won't be as bad as I remember, but it always is. I'm still stuck on, what the fuck happened? And in particular, was it our fault?

00:43:15.350 --> 00:43:26.330
<v Mike Comite>On stage, Rivers had made it seem like it. But I've gone through the details again and again. Julia had received tuning instructions from Weezer's road manager. Were those incorrect?

00:43:26.330 --> 00:43:40.730
<v Mike Comite>Had Julia done the math wrong tuning her uke? Had I? I'd helped her with that. I found a video of Julia rehearsing with Weezer on their tour bus. She was in the wrong tuning there, too, but no one had noticed?

00:43:40.730 --> 00:43:58.400
<v Mike Comite>Julia might know what went wrong, but I never really talked to her about that show. Only once, right after. It was uncomfortable. She was clearly upset, had been crying. She asked to let it go, so I did. But she must remember something from that day. So I called her.

00:43:58.400 --> 00:44:03.080
<v Mike Comite>When is the last time you thought about the Weezer performance?

00:44:03.080 --> 00:44:07.560
<v Julia Nunes>It doesn't, like, revisit me in the quiet, dark night.

00:44:07.560 --> 00:44:08.120
<v Mike Comite>OK.

00:44:08.120 --> 00:44:25.430
<v Julia Nunes>I think, when Weezer comes up, like if Weezer is on at a party, I might or might not be like, I've played with Weezer once, and I totally-- [LAUGHS] fumbled.

00:44:25.430 --> 00:44:36.450
<v Mike Comite>Julia lives in Austin, Texas now. She's still releasing music, but her career has shifted more towards life coaching and guided meditation. Her memories of the Weezer incident were not as vivid as mine.

00:44:36.450 --> 00:44:54.060
<v Mike Comite>She didn't remember how she and Weezer got in touch. She didn't remember what year it happened. She definitely did not have the email from the road manager about the tuning, and she never puzzled over why her tuning was off. Because for her, that whole show was a totally different experience. When Rivers was saying all that stuff about her on stage--

00:44:54.060 --> 00:45:01.430
<v Julia Nunes>It never registered to me as, like, anything-- anything other than a musician just trying to make the show go.

00:45:01.430 --> 00:45:10.710
<v Mike Comite>Oh, my god, I feel like I've just been stewing in it for so long, being like, that dude was an asshole to my friend.

00:45:10.710 --> 00:45:23.790
<v Julia Nunes>Oh, Mike! No way, man. I have never once thought that Rivers was mean. I didn't know what to say in that moment. I didn't know what to do. Like, he could have just been like, OK, never mind.

00:45:23.790 --> 00:45:25.130
<v Julia Nunes>[LAUGHTER]

00:45:25.130 --> 00:45:34.200
<v Julia Nunes>But the fact that he came up with a real-time solution to be like, yeah, just sing-- I was so grateful.

00:45:34.200 --> 00:46:00.000
<v Mike Comite>I watch this video probably more often than I should. [CHUCKLES] I feel like I'm responsible for it in a way that's like, if I had just not said anything in that rehearsal that day, we could have just gone about our days and rehearsed and then just had our set at Bonnaroo. And then you would have come to the Weezer set and sat in the bleachers with us, and we just would have enjoyed Weezer together.

00:46:00.000 --> 00:46:03.090
<v Julia Nunes>[GASPS] Mike, don't you dare.

00:46:03.090 --> 00:46:19.610
<v Mike Comite>But instead, you went on stage with him, and this thing that could have changed your career, in my view, had this effect that, you seemed so sad afterwards and devastated. And I was like, oh, my god, if I had just said nothing, this wouldn't have happened to you.

00:46:19.610 --> 00:46:55.920
<v Julia Nunes>God, I-- I don't think that experience had any sort of detrimental pivot for my career. The thing that made my career not happen is that I couldn't take the pressure. It's not that you-- I think that you offering that Weezer thing was brilliant. I wish I would have done a bunch of different things, but not playing with Weezer and not fucking up on stage is not one of my regrets at all.

00:46:55.920 --> 00:47:00.760
<v Mike Comite>[CHUCKLES] I'm glad. [SIGHS IN RELIEF]

00:47:00.760 --> 00:47:08.670
<v Mike Comite>[JULIA LAUGHS]

00:47:08.670 --> 00:47:21.600
<v Ira Glass>Julia was so much more at peace with the day than I was. She said the band, our band, wasn't sounding good to her anyways. She thought opening for Weezer was a long shot, even if she had been in the right tuning for "Tripping Down the Freeway."

00:47:21.600 --> 00:47:33.000
<v Ira Glass>But also, the moment Julia remembered most from that performance wasn't the mistake. It was the part of the video that I usually skip over, the moment where it works out, after Rivers asked Julia if she wants to sing.

00:47:33.000 --> 00:48:05.460
<v Julia Nunes>I'm looking Rivers Cuomo in the eye. Once we decide to start singing, everyone cheers. And we start, like, dancing together, and we put our arms around each other, and we're, like, head to head, singing, like, full blast, "Tripping Down the Freeway." And I felt like we sounded really good together. Yeah, I don't know. Like, all of that feels important to me.

00:48:05.460 --> 00:48:23.100
<v Mike Comite>You can't see any of this in the video I'd been replaying all these years. That one was shot from way back near the bleachers, where I watched the show. I almost couldn't believe what Julia described. But changing up the wording of my YouTube search, I found another video from that day, filmed close to the stage.

00:48:23.100 --> 00:48:33.270
<v Mike Comite>You can see everyone's faces, and even when the tuning mistake becomes apparent, they're smiling and they're laughing. Julia and Rivers dance. They're having a great time.

00:48:33.270 --> 00:48:49.620
<v Mike Comite>Watching it, I felt this wave of relief. It's what I was missing all these years. Julia was OK, and I believed her that the band didn't break up because of a tuning mistake. And this moment wasn't why I stopped playing music for a living.

00:48:49.620 --> 00:49:17.040
<v Mike Comite>After Julia and I talked, I finally heard back from Weezer's former road manager. She found the email they'd sent us before the performance, and the instructions were wrong. There would have been no way for Julia and me to tune correctly with them. I had the full answer now, but I was surprised by how little it mattered to me. I was over it.

00:49:17.040 --> 00:51:13.870
<v Ira Glass>Mike Comite. He is one of the super skilled people who work here at the show, doing audio mixes and adding music to our stories. Diane Wu produced this story. Here is Mike playing guitar and singing with Julia, whose full name, by the way, is Julia Nunes. This is a song they used to cover together, years ago.

00:51:13.870 --> 00:51:35.350
<v Ira Glass>Act Three, And If You See Her, Tell Her It's Over Now. In this last act, we turn from small, personal moments to big news that the whole world experiences, but that hits some people very, very personally. You probably saw the headlines and reports that a couple of weeks ago, after his family ruled Syria for over 50 years, the president/dictator Bashar Al Assad, was run out of his own country very suddenly.

00:51:35.350 --> 00:51:46.520
<v Ira Glass>Assad ran a government that did not tolerate dissent. He used chemical weapons against his own citizens. He spent much of the last 13 years brutally crushing an uprising. Hundreds of thousands of Syrians were killed, tortured, disappeared.

00:51:46.520 --> 00:52:05.470
<v Ira Glass>More than half the population was displaced in that conflict. Six million Syrians fled the country. So when a rebel coalition forced Assad out two weeks ago, Syrians all over the globe had their world turned upside down, and a few of us here at the show called around to see what that's been like for them. Diane Wu put together this story.

00:52:05.470 --> 00:52:20.470
<v Diane Wu>The regime collapsed late on a Saturday night. My coworkers and I talked to a few Syrians who are living abroad now about what that night was like for them. One was up studying for an exam. Another was out to an anniversary dinner. He kept checking his phone.

00:52:20.470 --> 00:52:37.010
<v Diane Wu>But the person I want to tell you about is Salma. She was in London on Saturday. She lives in another part of England. But watching the news by herself in the days before that, she felt like she had to be around other Syrians. So she got on the train and headed to her friend's apartment. She'd been crashing there since Thursday.

00:52:37.010 --> 00:52:58.420
<v Salma>It's like this tiny one-- it's not even an apartment. It's like a studio. So it's this tiny, tiny studio. We're all sitting together on this couch, five of us, and we're all, like, on our phones. And then the TV's on, and we're all checking, and there's barely any space. I don't know if I would describe it as crashing, because we didn't sleep. So--

00:52:58.420 --> 00:52:59.300
<v Diane Wu>(LAUGHING) OK.

00:52:59.300 --> 00:53:08.200
<v Salma>(LAUGHING) --none of us were sleeping, actually. It felt like we were on duty for some reason, like we were on call constantly.

00:53:08.200 --> 00:53:29.440
<v Diane Wu>It felt like their job to not look away. Something huge was happening back home. The rebels kept taking more and more ground each day, liberating more and more cities with hardly any pushback. Everyone was worried that Assad would do something desperate, like retaliate with chemical weapons or bombings, or that Russia would jump in. Salma and her friends were barely keeping it together.

00:53:29.440 --> 00:53:43.410
<v Salma>One of them-- it's not funny, but he kept fainting. And so-- [LAUGHS] he would go into the room, and then just, like, almost pass out. So the first thing I did was-- I didn't know what to do, so I gave him a tomato with salt on it, and I just shoved it into his mouth.

00:53:43.410 --> 00:53:44.810
<v Salma>[LAUGHTER]

00:53:44.810 --> 00:53:49.030
<v Salma>And I was like, OK, I think your blood pressure is dropping. And--

00:53:49.030 --> 00:53:56.020
<v Diane Wu>And were you guys like-- were you worried or freaked out, or was he kind of like, oh, this is something that happens?

00:53:56.020 --> 00:54:02.780
<v Salma>We were-- I don't think we were super freaked out, just because a lot of us have medical training. [CHUCKLES]

00:54:02.780 --> 00:54:03.970
<v Diane Wu>(LAUGHING) OK.

00:54:03.970 --> 00:54:05.950
<v Salma>It wasn't that big of a deal. And so--

00:54:05.950 --> 00:54:07.450
<v Diane Wu>That's handy. [LAUGHS] OK.

00:54:07.450 --> 00:54:08.380
<v Salma>Yeah.

00:54:08.380 --> 00:54:17.890
<v Diane Wu>They notice there is a pattern to his fainting. First, he'd start sweating profusely, then go stand in the door to cool off, then head towards the bathroom.

00:54:17.890 --> 00:54:27.860
<v Salma>By the third time, we kind of got the routine down. Like, we saw him open the door. We're like, he's about to pass out. Like, someone start, you know-- [LAUGHS] doing all the steps.

00:54:27.860 --> 00:54:35.810
<v Salma>We kept joking. They're like, we have to-- you can't pass out now. You got to be strong. You got to make it until the regime falls.

00:54:35.810 --> 00:54:53.120
<v Diane Wu>Salma told me that her friend who kept fainting had been detained by the regime when he was a teenager, three times. He fled Syria after the third time, but his parents are still there, and he was really concerned about them. Other people in the room were also having physical reactions from all the stress and fear.

00:54:53.120 --> 00:55:10.740
<v Salma>I think it was just, like, our bodies going into shock, and each person was kind of doing it differently. Like, for me, I cry a lot, and I have panic attacks, and then I throw up, which is kind of gross. But that's what would happen to me. Like, I would get really nauseous, really nauseous, really nauseous. Like, I would go and throw up.

00:55:10.740 --> 00:55:25.950
<v Diane Wu>It was from inside this crowded apartment, scattered with takeout containers and nervous bodies, that these friends then witnessed a sudden unraveling that none of them had anticipated. Salma is from Damascus, the capital, which was the seat of the Assad regime.

00:55:25.950 --> 00:55:50.520
<v Diane Wu>And as the rebels kept advancing across Syria, taking Hama, and then As-Suwayda, and then Homs, her friends from those cities celebrated around her. If the rebels succeeded, her hometown would be the last to fall. When Salma saw a video of people standing on a tank in Umayyad Square in Damascus, singing "Jannah Jannah," a revolutionary song, it was finally real to her. It was over.

00:55:50.520 --> 00:56:05.160
<v Salma>Knowing that we would be the last, I was holding it in. And so the first thing I did was, I cried, I hugged all my friends, I-- I sat there kind of staring at the wall, crying, crying, crying, crying.

00:56:05.160 --> 00:56:23.580
<v Diane Wu>They stayed up all night, and then celebrated more the next day in Trafalgar Square. Then Salma went home and mostly laid in bed in the dark for a few days, trying to make sense of this brand-new world. Salma's family had left Damascus in the first year of the war, 2011, when she was 15.

00:56:23.580 --> 00:56:38.270
<v Diane Wu>They moved to Connecticut, where she joined the soccer team and tried to do regular life while going to protests against the regime on weekends. Now, she had to figure out how to reverse this thing she's been doing since she was a teenager, separating herself from Syria.

00:56:38.270 --> 00:57:09.650
<v Salma>I knew I couldn't go back, with the regime there. And so I started slowly distancing myself from my memories. Like, before, I would post a lot of photos, saying, I miss Damascus, or I miss this, or I miss my house, and I miss that. And I stopped doing that, kind of on purpose. And even between myself when I'm alone, if I would remember something or if I would find myself kind of daydreaming, I would stop myself, and I wouldn't let myself go through with it.

00:57:09.650 --> 00:57:12.320
<v Diane Wu>What kind of daydream?

00:57:12.320 --> 00:57:19.850
<v Salma>Like, sometimes, I would daydream about my house and, like-- (CRYING) sorry.

00:57:19.850 --> 00:57:22.760
<v Diane Wu>It's OK.

00:57:22.760 --> 00:57:52.490
<v Salma>Yeah, I don't know. Like, it's the place where I have a lot of good memories, and-- and it's the place where me and my siblings did this and did that. And sometimes, I would just daydream about walking into my room and going back and sitting in my living room and looking out the window. And I just-- I wouldn't let myself do that anymore. And even at times where I would have dreams about being in my house again, I would wake myself up and be like, no, this isn't real.

00:57:52.490 --> 00:58:06.020
<v Diane Wu>Someone else I talked to described it like this. Syria was on a different planet from the one he lived on now. There was no way to visit it. He had to flip a page, start a new life. Better not to think about it anymore.

00:58:06.020 --> 00:58:26.390
<v Diane Wu>But now that the regime was suddenly gone, Syria was back on this planet, a place like any other place. And they had to reset their minds to take that in, which was hard to do after so many years of doing the opposite. Salma started thinking about visiting home, not just in a dream way, but the logistics of where she would stay when she went back.

00:58:26.390 --> 00:58:35.990
<v Salma>So we still have our house now. I'm like, would I stay at my house, or would I want to stay somewhere else? Or what am I going to do? And now, I can think of all the plans.

00:58:35.990 --> 00:59:00.090
<v Diane Wu>The euphoria of the regime falling was laced with heavy feelings, too. In the days following the collapse, as Salma learned just how many people who'd been disappeared by the Assad government had been killed were not coming home, she had another panic attack.

00:59:00.090 --> 00:59:07.990
<v Diane Wu>Salma's been watching all kinds of videos coming out of the new Syria, and there's a particular type that delights her-- one I wasn't expecting.

00:59:07.990 --> 00:59:28.430
<v Salma>It was a girl in a karate uniform. And this guy was standing across from her with something on his head-- I can't remember-- like a water bottle. And she closes her eyes, and she, like, karate-kicks the water bottle off the top of his head with her eyes closed. And then the camera pans back, and everyone's, like, clapping and cheering.

00:59:28.430 --> 00:59:29.160
<v Diane Wu>Uh-huh.

00:59:29.160 --> 00:59:36.070
<v Salma>I saw people doing parkour in Damascus. They're doing, like, back flips in the street in the middle of the celebration.

00:59:36.070 --> 00:59:42.810
<v Diane Wu>Watching these people just be silly and happy-- for Salma, she sees that as getting to watch them finally be free.

00:59:42.810 --> 00:59:45.010
<v Salma>In Latakia, someone was lifting weights.

00:59:45.010 --> 00:59:46.360
<v Diane Wu>Like, in the street?

00:59:46.360 --> 01:00:04.540
<v Salma>Like, in the street. Yeah, like, in the middle of this. There's fighters, kind of, passing by on cars, waving flowers, and he's right on the side, doing all of these moves-- [LAUGHS] in his, like, gym clothes. It's just so, so unserious, so fun.

01:00:04.540 --> 01:00:27.700
<v Salma>It's things you could have done before, but it's just the mentality of, you're free, you can do anything, and you belong to this country, or it feels like it's yours again. I think the slogans of the regime were so damaging to our psyche, like calling it Suria al Asad-- Assad's Syria-- just-- it removes you from the equation.

01:00:27.700 --> 01:00:45.300
<v Salma>So who are you in Assad's Syria? You're nobody. You don't belong. Seeing the people now in Syria, seeing their reaction, they're slowly kind of feeling like it is theirs-- this is our country. We're the ones who are responsible for it now. We're the ones who are going to take care of it.

01:00:45.300 --> 01:01:04.530
<v Diane Wu>There's pretty much no way to overstate how much there is to do next, how many things will need to be figured out, how many unknowns there are. But one person told me, none of it could be worse than what we lived through already.

01:01:04.530 --> 01:01:42.150
<v Ira Glass>Diane Wu is a producer on our show. This story was co-produced by Hany Hawasly.

01:01:42.150 --> 01:01:46.200
<v Ira Glass>That's Mike and Julia covering Weezer's Sweater Song, "Undone."

01:01:46.200 --> 01:02:01.360
<v Ira Glass>Our program was produced today by Lilly Sullivan. The people who put together today's show include Phia Bennin, Dana Chivvis, Sean Cole, Cassie Howie, Chana Joffe-Walt, Henry Larson, Seth Lind, Katherine Rae Mondo, Stowe Nelson, Nadia Reiman, Anthony Roman, Ryan Rumery, Alissa Shipp, Lilly Sullivan, Christopher Swetala, and Matt Tierney.

01:02:01.360 --> 01:02:29.800
<v Ira Glass>Our managing editor, Sarah Abdurrahman. Our senior editor is David Kestenbaum. Our executive editor is Emanuele Berry. Special thanks today to Natalie Sullivan, Kim Sullivan, Sarah Kim, Steve Sopchak, Erin Maree Comite, Dave Burns, Todd Johnson, Leanne Victorine, Darien Woods, and Yazan Abou Ismail. To become a This American Life Partner, which gets you bonus content, ad-free listening, and hundreds of our favorite episodes of the shown in your podcast feed, go to thisamericanlife.org/lifepartners. That link is also in the show notes.

